UID:1eaaa810b11b8525@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260822T232151Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20160912
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine’s Bureau of Financial Institutions Wa
 rns Public about  Phony Text Messages Sent to Bank and Credit Union Custom
 ers
DESCRIPTION:Maine’s Bureau of Financial Institutions Warns Public about 
  Phony Text Messages Sent to Bank and Credit Union Customers\nSep 12\, 201
 6\nGARDINER –  Maine’s Bureau of Financial Institutions at the Depart
 ment of Professional and Financial Regulation warns of a scam involving fr
 audulent text messages.  The messages appear to come from a consumer’s 
 bank or credit union and indicate that there is a problem with the consume
 r’s account or debit card.  The scammers urgently request account and o
 ther personal information in order to fix the non-existent problem.  The 
 Bureau of Financial Institutions reminds consumers to look out for this sc
 am and not to divulge bank or credit union account numbers or other person
 al information by text\, phone or email.\nThe customers of several banks a
 nd credit unions received the text message.  The message does not contain
  the name of any particular institution and appears randomly sent to cell 
 phone numbers without targeting consumers at any particular institution. 
  “Banks and credit unions will not text\, call\, or email customers aski
 ng them to divulge account numbers\, pins or social security numbers\,” 
 Superintendent Lloyd P. LaFountain III said.\nLaFountain emphasized that c
 ustomers receiving unexpected calls\, e-mails or text messages should call
  their bank or credit union directly and talk to an employee.  He also no
 ted that customers should always be vigilant to protect their personal inf
 ormation and monitor account statements.\nIf a consumer suspects he or she
  has received a scam text\, the consumer should:\nNot return the text or c
 all the number provided\nNever provide personal account information or oth
 er personal information in response to a text\, call or e-mail.  A bank o
 r credit union does not request personal account information in such a man
 ner.\nThe Bureau of Financial Institutions has a consumer library with hel
 pful information about how consumers can spot and avoid financial scams. 
  Also\, the Bureau’s Consumer Outreach Specialist is av\nhttps://www.mai
 ne.gov/pfr/financialinstitutions/news-notices/2022-05-19-maines-bureau-fin
 ancial-institutions-warns-public-about-phony-text-messages
URL:https://www.maine.gov/pfr/financialinstitutions/news-notices/2022-05-1
 9-maines-bureau-financial-institutions-warns-public-about-phony-text-messa
 ges
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4d206fce5e3c9e49@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260822T232021Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260701
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Process for Appointment as a Bail Commissioner
DESCRIPTION:MAINE JUDICIAL BRANCH \nADA Notice: The Maine Judicial Branch 
 complies with the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). If you need a rea
 sonable \naccommodation\, contact the Court Access Coordinator\, accessibi
 lity@courts.maine.gov\, or a court clerk. \nLanguage Services: For languag
 e assistance and interpreters\, contact a court clerk or interpreters@cour
 ts.maine.gov. \n \nCR-288\, Rev. 07/26 Page 1 of 1 www.courts.maine.gov \n
 Process for Appointment as a Bail Commissioner   \n \n \nPROCESS FOR APPOI
 NTMENT AS A BAIL COMMISSIONER \n \nBail Commissioners are appointed by the
  Chief Judge of the District Court and serve at the pleasure of the \nCour
 t. They must be available to work on an irregular on-call schedule includi
 ng nights\, weekends\, and \nholidays. Appointments are made after review 
 of an application\, reference calls\, a background investigation\, \nand a
  determination by the Chief Judge as to the need for a Bail Commissioner i
 n the area where the applicant \nlives. \n \nPersons who are currently emp
 loyed by\, or have a family member currently employed by\, the following \
 nagencies/businesses\, are not eligible to apply: \n• A Law enforcement 
 agency\; \n• A Jail/correctional facility\; \n• A Law firm\; \n• The
  Department of Corrections\; \n• A Prosecutor’s office\; \n• A regio
 nal communications/dispatch center\; and/or \n• A Town/City\, County\, S
 tate\, or Federal government agency that has a law enforcement \nagency un
 der its supervision. \nIf appointed as a Bail Commissioner\, each applican
 t must do the following before being allowed to begin their \nduties: \n
 • Attend a mandatory training\; \n• Pass a fingerprint-based FBI crimi
 nal history background check\; \n• Complete an online certification to v
 iew Criminal Justice Information\; \n• Job shadow an experienced bail co
 mmissioner\; and \n• Open a dedicated bail commissioner bank account. \n
  \nBail Commissioners are independent contractors and are not State employ
 ees. The Judicial Branch does \nnot compensate Bail Commissioners for thei
 r time nor expenses. Rather\, Ba\nhttps://mjbportal.courts.maine.gov/Court
 Forms/FormsLists/DownloadForm?strFormNumber=CR-288
URL:https://mjbportal.courts.maine.gov/CourtForms/FormsLists/DownloadForm?
 strFormNumber=CR-288
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
